**A bean scope controls how many instances** Spring creates and **how long they live**.

**Spring Core scopes**
- **`singleton`** (default) — one instance per `ApplicationContext`, shared by all consumers
- **`prototype`** — a new instance every time the bean is requested (container stops managing it after construction)

**Web-aware scopes** (need a web application context)
- **`request`** — one instance per HTTP request
- **`session`** — one instance per HTTP session
- **`application`** — one per `ServletContext` (effectively singleton lifetime, but tied to web app)
- **`websocket`** — one per WebSocket session

**Additional**
- **`@RefreshScope`** (Spring Cloud) — bean recreated when configuration changes
- **`thread`** — custom scope via `SimpleThreadScope`
- **Custom scopes** via implementing `Scope`

**Declaring a scope**

```java
@Component
@Scope("prototype")
public class MyWorker { }

@Component
@RequestScope
public class RequestTracker { }

@Bean
@Scope(value = "prototype")
public ThingProcessor thingProcessor() { ... }
```

**The problem**

```java
@Component @Scope("prototype")
class Worker { }

@Service
class OrderService {
    @Autowired Worker worker;

    void handle() {
        worker.doStuff();
    }
}
```

- `@Autowired` runs at bean construction
- Singleton `OrderService` gets one `Worker` instance reused forever
- Defeats prototype behavior

**Fix 1: `ObjectProvider<T>` (recommended)**

```java
@Service
class OrderService {
    private final ObjectProvider<Worker> workers;

    OrderService(ObjectProvider<Worker> workers) { this.workers = workers; }

    void handle() {
        Worker worker = workers.getObject();
        worker.doStuff();
    }
}
```

**Fix 2: `Provider<T>` (JSR-330)**

```java
@Autowired Provider<Worker> workers;
Worker w = workers.get();
```

**Fix 3: `@Lookup` (method injection)**

```java
@Service
abstract class OrderService {
    @Lookup
    abstract Worker newWorker();

    void handle() { newWorker().doStuff(); }
}
```

- Spring subclasses the service and implements `newWorker()` to return a fresh prototype
- Rare in modern code

**Fix 4: Scoped proxy on the prototype**

```java
@Component
@Scope(value = "prototype", proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)
class Worker { }
```

- Injecting the prototype gives you a proxy; each method call fetches a new underlying instance
- Works but `ObjectProvider` is usually cleaner

**Same pattern for request/session-scoped beans**
- Use scoped proxy (`proxyMode = TARGET_CLASS`) when injecting them into singletons
- The singleton holds a proxy that dispatches to the right per-request / per-session instance

**Use `@Component` (or `@Service` / `@Repository` / `@Controller`) when**

- You own the class and can edit its source
- Construction is straightforward — just inject dependencies via constructor
- You want one instance with the default name (class name camelCased)
- The class is part of your application's domain or service layer

**Example — typical service**

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository repo;
    private final PaymentClient payments;

    public OrderService(OrderRepository repo, PaymentClient payments) {
        this.repo = repo;
        this.payments = payments;
    }
}
```

**What Spring does**
- Component scan finds `OrderService` in the package
- Spring uses its single constructor
- Resolves `OrderRepository` and `PaymentClient` from the container
- Registers the new instance as a bean named `orderService`

**`@Component` benefits**
- **Less boilerplate** — no factory method
- **Convention** — the class declares its own role
- **Discoverable** — `grep` for `@Service` finds your business logic

**`@Component` limitations**
- Can't be applied to classes you don't own (third-party libraries)
- Limited construction control — Spring picks the constructor and arguments
- Hard to register multiple instances of the same type without subclassing or qualifiers

**Use `@Bean` when**

- The class is from a third-party library (you can't add `@Component`)
- Construction requires logic — builder patterns, conditionals, environment-dependent values
- You need multiple instances of the same type with different configs
- You're integrating an existing factory or static method
- You want explicit control for testability or readability

**Example — third-party class**

```java
@Configuration
public class HttpConfig {
    @Bean
    public RestTemplate restTemplate(RestTemplateBuilder builder) {
        return builder
            .setConnectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(2))
            .setReadTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5))
            .build();
    }
}
```

- `RestTemplate` is from Spring Web — you can't add `@Component` to it
- The `@Bean` method gives you explicit timeout and builder control

**Example — multiple beans of same type**

```java
@Configuration
public class DataSourceConfig {
    @Bean @Primary
    public DataSource primaryDataSource() {
        return DataSourceBuilder.create().url("jdbc:postgresql://primary/app").build();
    }

    @Bean
    public DataSource readonlyDataSource() {
        return DataSourceBuilder.create().url("jdbc:postgresql://replica/app").build();
    }
}
```

- Two `DataSource` beans with different configs
- `@Primary` decides the default; `@Qualifier("readonlyDataSource")` selects the other

**`@Bean` and CGLIB proxying — the inter-method call rule**
- `@Configuration` classes are subclassed by CGLIB at runtime
- Calling one `@Bean` method from another (`primaryDataSource()` inside another `@Bean` method) returns the **same singleton instance**, not a new construction
- This is what allows safe inter-bean wiring through method calls
- Without `@Configuration` (just `@Component` on the config class), the proxy isn't applied — each method call constructs a fresh instance, breaking singleton semantics

**`@Autowired` is the Spring-native injection annotation** and the one used most often in Spring codebases.

**Resolution algorithm**
- Match by **type** — find all beans assignable to the field's declared type
- If exactly one match, inject it
- If multiple matches, look for a bean whose name matches the field name
- If still ambiguous, fail unless `@Qualifier("specificName")` is present
- If zero matches and `required = true` (default), throw `NoSuchBeanDefinitionException`; if `required = false`, leave null

**Constructor injection (recommended)**

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository repo;

    public OrderService(OrderRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }
}
```

- Since Spring 4.3, `@Autowired` is **optional on a single constructor**
- Allows `final` fields — immutable dependencies
- Best for testability — construct manually with mocks

**Field injection (discouraged but common)**

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Autowired
    private OrderRepository repo;
}
```

- Concise but harder to test
- Can't use `final`
- Hides dependencies from constructor signature

**Setter injection**

```java
@Autowired
public void setRepo(OrderRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }
```

- Useful for optional dependencies and circular reference workarounds

**Optional with `required = false`**

```java
@Autowired(required = false)
private MetricsCollector metrics;
```

- Field is null if no matching bean exists; check before use

**`@Inject` is the JSR-330 standard** — annotated code can run on any JSR-330-compliant DI container, not just Spring.

**When portability matters**
- Library code that should work in Spring, Guice, CDI, or Dagger applications
- Hexagonal architecture where the application core should not depend on a specific framework
- Open-source modules with multiple consumer ecosystems

**Same syntax as `@Autowired`, different package**

```java
import jakarta.inject.Inject;

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository repo;

    @Inject
    public OrderService(OrderRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }
}
```

**Disambiguation with `@Named`**

```java
@Inject
public OrderService(@Named("primary") DataSource ds) { }
```

- JSR-330 equivalent of `@Qualifier`
- Works the same way at runtime

**Optional and lazy dependencies — no `required` flag**
- `@Inject Optional<Foo> foo;` — null-safe optional
- `@Inject Provider<Foo> fooProvider;` — lazy / per-call resolution (JSR-330 equivalent of Spring's `ObjectProvider`)

**Practical guidance**
- For a Spring-only project, `@Autowired` is more idiomatic
- For shared library code or portable modules, `@Inject` is the right choice
- Mixing both in the same class works but is confusing — pick one per project
